<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theophilus I of Alexandria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 412</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theophilus was Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> from 385 to 412. With imperial backing he led the campaign against paganism in the city, and in 391 the Serapeum, its greatest temple, was destroyed. He also helped depose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-chrysostom">John Chrysostom</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, at the Synod of the Oak in 403, and was succeeded by his nephew Cyril.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Theophilus's early life, though he was probably born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the mid-4th century. He rose to the patriarchate in a time of change in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. Christianity had recently gained imperial support through the policies of Constantine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a>, displacing long-dominant pagan cults, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, a centre of learning and philosophical traditions, became a focus of religious and political tension. He succeeded Pope Timothy I as Patriarch of Alexandria in 385, the twenty-third Pope of Alexandria, and mentored his nephew Cyril of Alexandria, who later succeeded him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 391, after imperial edicts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> banned pagan worship and closed temples across the empire, Theophilus led efforts to repurpose or dismantle pagan religious structures in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. According to the church historian Socrates Scholasticus, he publicly mocked pagan artefacts, which provoked violent clashes between pagan and Christian factions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serapeum-of-alexandria">Serapeum of Alexandria</a>, one of the most prominent temples of the city, was destroyed. Christian sources described it as a victory over idolatry, while modern scholars note that it reflected wider political, religious and social tensions and was part of a campaign to consolidate Christian authority and imperial control.</p><p>Theophilus at first supported the allegorical theology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/origen">Origen</a> but in 399 changed his position and sided with the Anthropomorphite monks, who read divine imagery literally. At a synod in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> he condemned Origen's writings and expelled Origenist monks, among them the Four Tall Brothers, who took refuge with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-chrysostom">John Chrysostom</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. In 403 Theophilus presided over the Synod of the Oak in Constantinople, which deposed Chrysostom, whom he accused of insubordination and heresy, partly for sheltering the exiled Origenists.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Theophilus died on 15 October 412 and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. He was a prolific author, but most of his writings survive only in fragments or translations: festal letters announcing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a>, homilies including one on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crucifixion">Crucifixion</a> and the Good Thief, and letters exchanged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a>, Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasius-i-dicorus">Anastasius I</a> and Pope Innocent I. His paschal table, dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a>, is lost. Some of his prayers were translated into Geʽez. He is venerated as a saint in the Coptic Orthodox, Eastern Orthodox Church and the Syriac Orthodox Church, with a feast day on 18 Paopi in the Coptic calendar, 28 October.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Theophilus I of Alexandria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27,29&amp;year=402" aria-label="Egypt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5313 1173 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Theophilus I of Alexandria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5763 1443h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5763 1443h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=27,29&amp;year=402">What was here in 402 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
